WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.629 --> 00:00:09.340
This video highlights new and existing view
options for Alignment tracks in the GDV, NCBI's

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.040
Genome Data Viewer.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.849
We'll focus on BAM alignment data that are
hosted remotely, and how to view haplotype

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:20.869
structural variation, but many of the display
options you'll see also apply to viewing other

00:00:20.869 --> 00:00:25.670
types of data, such as BLAST results and SRA
data.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:33.090
I'll start on the GDV home page and for this
demo change to the GRCh37 assembly and click

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:36.230
Browse Genome.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:41.910
To load the remote BAM file, use the User
Data and Track Hubs menu.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:45.880
I'll first point out the Supported File and
Data types.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:50.900
Notice that for remote BAM and VCF files,
the directory you point to must contain the

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.260
corresponding index files.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:01:01.140
Okay, I'll select Options, add Remote Files,
and paste in the URL for my file, and I'll

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:07.090
give the new track a name, trio, and click
Add.

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:10.619
You see a warning, but the data were added
successfully.

00:01:10.619 --> 00:01:15.859
Warnings like these report incongruities between
the file and the genome assembly, for instance

00:01:15.859 --> 00:01:20.399
that the file contains extra sequences that
aren't in the assembly.

00:01:20.399 --> 00:01:23.719
Details has more specific information.

00:01:23.719 --> 00:01:28.219
Your added tracks are placed at the bottom,
but you can move any track by click and drag

00:01:28.219 --> 00:01:30.399
in the tracks title bar.

00:01:30.399 --> 00:01:36.310
I'm going to clean up a bit by using the red
X on the right to delete some tracks.

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:39.729
You might also notice that the added track
is shaded green.

00:01:39.729 --> 00:01:45.259
This differentiates user added tracks from
those provided by NCBI.

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:50.290
At this zoom level we're seeing all of chromosome
1, and the coverage graph of reads in our

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:54.829
trio track appears to show good, even coverage
across the chromosome.

00:01:54.829 --> 00:01:57.460
This big gap is the centromere region.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:02.329
Let's zoom in a bit and display specific,
100 kilobase region by typing in the Search

00:02:02.329 --> 00:02:03.619
widget on the left.

00:02:03.619 --> 00:02:12.770
I want to go to chr1: 69,900K-70,000K.

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:17.650
We're getting more detail at this level, where
red denotes areas with more mismatches and

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:21.520
black denotes indel variation in the read
coverage.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:31.420
In the region 69,940K to 69,945K, notice the
dips, suggesting deletions relative to the

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:33.110
genome reference.

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:36.030
I'll zoom to this region.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:40.660
We see two regions where most of the reads
have these deletions or gaps.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:44.540
I'll spend the next couple minutes adjusting
various track settings to explore some of

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:47.120
the display options available.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.960
You can use the gear icon next to the delete
icon in the track title, or the one in the

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.500
tooltip.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.900
I'll click on the one in the tooltip and change
a few settings.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:03.140
I'll check the box to Show pileup, keeping
the Pileup Display set to Match/Mismatch graph

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:09.540
(count); in the Hide Alignments menu I'll
choose to hide both duplicates and bad reads;

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:15.650
in the Sort alignments by menu, I'll choose
Haplotype, then hit Apply.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:21.710
The pileup graph is just below the track title,
and in this Match/Mismatch graph (count) display

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:24.500
clearly marks the two indel regions.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:32.520
The reads are now clustered by haplotype;
haplotype 1, haplotype 2, and haplotype not

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.010
set.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:38.710
Notice that haplotype 2 does not have the
deletions seen in haplotype 1.

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:45.910
Let's use highlight and zoom to sequence around
the smaller indel region on the left side.

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:52.520
At this level, all the mismatches, deletions
(the dark red lines) and insertions (the blue

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:54.490
bars) are apparent.

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:58.640
The length of an insertion correlates to size
of the blue bar.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:02.970
Okay, let's look at one of the other pileup
display options.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:09.360
I'll go back into track settings and change
Pileup Display to Match/mismatch table (count),

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.580
but you see that other options are available.

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:14.510
I'll hit Apply.

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:20.350
The pileup table now indicates the individual
read counts for a match, mismatch, gap, or

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:23.320
intron; refer to the legend on the left.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:26.710
In addition, higher counts are shaded more
darkly.

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:29.900
You see this easily in the gap region.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:34.250
Now let's take a look at a read's tooltip,
there is a lot of good information there.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:39.750
A mouseover on a read's row activates the
tooltip, and I'll click on this pushpin to

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.410
keep it open.

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:43.600
So here we can get,
orientation

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:47.550
the span of that read on the reference molecule,
chr 1 in our case

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:52.970
% coverage, and so on
You also get a CIGAR string

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:58.590
And at the bottom you have a button called,
View, to see any unaligned regions.

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:03.750
Here the unaligned reads are in red, and you
can copy/paste this sequence for further analysis,

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:09.690
e.g., to see if this is vector sequence, or
part of a translocation or chimera?

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:13.790
Be aware that this tooltip information is
also present for other types of alignment

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:19.220
data, such as BAM files for expression data
or for BLAST data.

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:24.150
The last example is a quick look for NCBI
RefSNPs in the region of structural variation

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:25.170
we've seen here.

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:32.340
I want to add a track of those RefSNPs, so
I'll go to the Tracks menu, Configure Tracks,

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:35.900
and show the Variation tracks we can choose
from.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:41.900
Let's go to the most recent dbSNP build, 153,
and select Live RefSNPs.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:45.810
I'll hit Configure.

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:51.889
Here is the added RefSNPs track, and there
is a RefSNP in this region, the rs ending

00:05:51.889 --> 00:05:53.550
in 8066.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:57.330
In the tooltip for the rs, you get a link
to the SNP record where you can investigate

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:00.389
this further.

00:06:00.389 --> 00:06:02.010
That's all for this video.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:06.510
We'd love to have your questions and feedback,
so please use either the Feedback form in

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:12.450
the Help menu, or the Support Center link
near the bottom of most NCBI web pages.

